(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the EMF equation of a single-phase transformer, and explain the physical significance of mutual magnetic flux.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   EMF Equation and Flux Linkage in a Single-Phase Transformer:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - A transformer consists of two magnetically coupled, electrically isolated windings \(primary) Tj
T*
(   with N1 turns, secondary with N2 turns\) wound upon a common high-permeability laminated) Tj
T*
(   ferromagnetic core.) Tj
T*
(   - Alternating voltage v1\(t\) applied to the primary establishes an alternating magnetizing) Tj
T*
(   current that drives sinusoidal mutual magnetic flux in the core:) Tj
T*
(   Phi\(t\) = Phi_m * sin\(omega * t\), where Phi_m = B_m * A_core.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation of Induced EMF:) Tj
T*
(   - According to Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction, the instantaneous EMF induced in) Tj
T*
(   primary winding:) Tj
T*
(   e1\(t\) = - N1 * d\(Phi\)/dt = - N1 * d/dt [ Phi_m * sin\(omega * t\) ] = - N1 * omega * Phi_m *) Tj
T*
(   cos\(omega * t\).) Tj
T*
(   - Using trigonometric identity -cos\(theta\) = sin\(theta - pi/2\):) Tj
T*
(   e1\(t\) = N1 * omega * Phi_m * sin\(omega * t - pi/2\).) Tj
T*
(   - The induced EMF lags the mutual flux by strictly 90 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   - Peak value of induced EMF:) Tj
T*
(   E_m1 = N1 * omega * Phi_m = N1 * \(2 * pi * f\) * Phi_m.) Tj
T*
(   - RMS Value of Primary Induced EMF:) Tj
T*
(   E1 = E_m1 / sqrt\(2\) = \(2 * pi / sqrt\(2\)\) * f * N1 * Phi_m = sqrt\(2\) * pi * f * N1 * Phi_m) Tj
T*
(   approx 4.44 * f * N1 * Phi_m.) Tj
T*
(   - Similarly, for the secondary winding of N2 turns:) Tj
T*
(   E2 = 4.44 * f * N2 * Phi_m.) Tj
T*
(   3. Transformation Ratio:) Tj
T*
(   - E2 / E1 = N2 / N1 = K.) Tj
T*
(   - For an ideal transformer \(zero leakage flux and zero winding resistance\): V2 / V1 = E2 / E1 =) Tj
T*
(   N2 / N1.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Develop the complete equivalent circuit model of a practical transformer, explaining how winding resistances and leakage reactances are referred between sides.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Equivalent Circuit Model of Practical Transformer:) Tj
T*
(   1. Deviations from Ideal Transformer:) Tj
T*
(   - Primary and secondary winding copper resistances: R1 and R2.) Tj
T*
(   - Magnetic leakage fluxes linking only one winding without traversing the mutual core: primary) Tj
T*
(   leakage reactance X1 and secondary leakage reactance X2.) Tj
T*
(   - Core losses: Hysteresis and eddy currents modeled by shunt resistance Rc \(or R0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Magnetizing MMF required to drive core flux modeled by shunt magnetizing reactance Xm \(or) Tj
T*
(   X0\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Shunt Core Excitation Branch \(No-Load Branch\):) Tj
T*
(   - At no load, transformer draws small no-load current I0 \(approx 2-5% of rated full-load) Tj
T*
(   current\).) Tj
T*
(   - Resolves into two orthogonal quadrature components:) Tj
T*
(   * Core loss component: Iw = I0 * cos\(phi0\), flowing through shunt core resistance Rc = V1 / Iw.) Tj
T*
(   * Magnetizing component: Im = I0 * sin\(phi0\), flowing through shunt magnetizing reactance Xm =) Tj
T*
(   V1 / Im.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
