(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Speed Control methods for DC Shunt Motors \(Armature Resistance, Field Weakening, and Ward-Leonard System\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Speed Control Techniques for DC Shunt Motors:) Tj
T*
(   1. Governing Speed Equation:) Tj
T*
(   N = [ \(V - Ia * Ra\) / \(k * Phi\) ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Armature Resistance Control \(Rheostatic Method\):) Tj
T*
(   - External variable rheostat R_ext inserted in series with armature circuit.) Tj
T*
(   - Speed: N = [ V - Ia * \(Ra + R_ext\) ] / \(k * Phi\).) Tj
T*
(   - As R_ext increases, voltage drop across armature increases, dropping speed below base \(rated\)) Tj
T*
(   speed.) Tj
T*
(   - Drawbacks: Inefficient \(large I^2*R_ext power loss as heat\), poor speed regulation under) Tj
T*
(   varying load.) Tj
T*
(   3. Field Flux Control \(Field Weakening Method\):) Tj
T*
(   - Variable rheostat inserted in series with shunt field winding.) Tj
T*
(   - Increasing rheostat resistance reduces shunt field current If and field flux Phi.) Tj
T*
(   - Speed N is inversely proportional to Phi; hence motor accelerates ABOVE rated base speed.) Tj
T*
(   - Advantages: Highly efficient \(field current is small ~1-2 A\), smooth control. Limited at high) Tj
T*
(   speed by commutation sparking.) Tj
T*
(   4. Ward-Leonard System \(Armature Voltage Control\):) Tj
T*
(   - Setup: DC motor armature fed directly from a dedicated DC generator driven by an AC induction) Tj
T*
(   motor.) Tj
T*
(   - Armature voltage is smoothly adjusted from zero to rated maximum by varying the low-power) Tj
T*
(   field rheostat of the DC generator.) Tj
T*
(   - Reversing generator field polarity seamlessly reverses motor rotation without high-current) Tj
T*
(   contactors.) Tj
T*
(   - Advantages: Wide speed range \(zero to rated in both directions\), highly sensitive, excellent) Tj
T*
(   regulation. High initial capital cost.) Tj
T*
