(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the Torque-Slip Equation of a 3-phase Induction Motor, and analyze operating regions \(motoring, generating, braking\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Torque-Slip Characteristics and Operating Modes of 3-Phase Induction Motors:) Tj
T*
(   1. Torque Equation Derivation:) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor induced EMF at standstill: E2. At slip s, rotor induced EMF: E2s = s * E2.) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor winding resistance: R2. Standstill leakage reactance: X2. At slip s: X2s = s * X2.) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor impedance per phase: Z2s = sqrt\( R2^2 + \(s * X2\)^2 \).) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor phase current: I2s = \(s * E2\) / sqrt\( R2^2 + \(s * X2\)^2 \).) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor power factor: cos\(phi2\) = R2 / sqrt\( R2^2 + \(s * X2\)^2 \).) Tj
T*
(   - Developed electromagnetic torque:) Tj
T*
(   T proportional to E2 * I2s * cos\(phi2\) = [ k * s * E2^2 * R2 ] / [ R2^2 + \(s * X2\)^2 ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Peak \(Breakdown\) Torque and Slip:) Tj
T*
(   - Maximizing T with respect to slip s:) Tj
T*
(   dT / ds = 0 => s_max = R2 / X2.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum Torque Magnitude:) Tj
T*
(   T_max = [ k * E2^2 ] / [ 2 * X2 ].) Tj
T*
(   - Notice: Peak torque T_max is completely independent of rotor resistance R2! Adding external) Tj
T*
(   rotor resistance shifts the peak torque toward higher slip without diminishing its magnitude.) Tj
T*
(   3. Three Distinct Operating Regimes:) Tj
T*
