(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Blondel's Two-Reaction Theory for Salient-Pole Synchronous Machines, deriving the power-angle relation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Blondel's Two-Reaction Theory for Salient-Pole Machines:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Basis:) Tj
T*
(   - Cylindrical rotor machines have uniform air-gap reluctance around rotor periphery.) Tj
T*
(   - Salient-pole machines have non-uniform air-gap: minimum air-gap along direct pole axis) Tj
T*
(   \(d-axis\), maximum air-gap along interpolar quadrature axis \(q-axis\).) Tj
T*
(   - Armature current Ia is resolved into two orthogonal components:) Tj
T*
(   * Direct-axis component: Id = Ia * sin\(delta + phi\), acting along d-axis where reluctance is) Tj
T*
(   low \(reactance is high: Xd\).) Tj
T*
(   * Quadrature-axis component: Iq = Ia * cos\(delta + phi\), acting along q-axis where reluctance) Tj
T*
(   is high \(reactance is low: Xq < Xd\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Phasor Voltage Equation:) Tj
T*
(   - Terminal voltage: V /_ 0 deg = Ef /_ delta - j * Id * Xd - j * Iq * Xq - Ia * Ra.) Tj
T*
(   3. Power-Angle Equation \(neglecting Ra\):) Tj
T*
(   - Active power output per phase:) Tj
T*
(   P = V * Ia * cos\(phi\) = V * [ Id * sin\(delta\) + Iq * cos\(delta\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting Id = \(Ef - V * cos delta\) / Xd and Iq = \(V * sin delta\) / Xq:) Tj
T*
(   P = [ \(V * Ef\) / Xd ] * sin\(delta\) + [ V^2 * \(Xd - Xq\) / \(2 * Xd * Xq\) ] * sin\(2 * delta\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Physical Significance:) Tj
T*
(   - First Term: Electromagnetic field power \(fundamental excitation power\).) Tj
T*
(   - Second Term: Reluctance Power, arising purely from rotor saliency \(Xd != Xq\). Operates at) Tj
T*
(   double angle sin\(2*delta\), allowing machine to maintain synchronism even if field excitation) Tj
T*
(   fails completely \(Ef = 0\)!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
