BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Switched Reluctance Motors \(SRM\), covering torque production, stator/rotor pole configurations, and converters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Switched Reluctance Motor \(SRM\) Systems:) Tj
T*
(   1. Machine Construction:) Tj
T*
(   - Doubly salient machine: Salient poles on both stator and rotor.) Tj
T*
(   - Stator carries simple concentrated phase coils.) Tj
T*
(   - Rotor is a single piece of stamped laminated steel with zero windings, zero permanent) Tj
T*
(   magnets, and zero slip rings/brushes \(extraordinarily rugged and cost-effective\).) Tj
T*
(   - Common pole configurations: 6/4 \(6 stator poles, 4 rotor poles\) or 8/6.) Tj
T*
(   2. Principle of Torque Production:) Tj
T*
(   - Operates on the fundamental variable reluctance principle: magnetic flux lines strive to) Tj
T*
(   minimize reluctance \(maximize inductance\).) Tj
T*
(   - When stator phase is energized, the rotor pole is pulled toward alignment with the stator) Tj
T*
(   pole.) Tj
T*
(   - Electromagnetic Torque: T = 0.5 * i^2 * \(dL / dtheta\).) Tj
T*
(   - Torque is independent of current polarity \(always pulls toward alignment\); unipolar current) Tj
T*
(   drives are used.) Tj
T*
(   3. Power Electronic Converter:) Tj
T*
(   - Asymmetric Bridge Converter \(Two switches and two freewheeling diodes per phase\).) Tj
T*
(   - Provides shoot-through fault immunity since phase winding is in series with both switches.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
