12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Transformer Protection: Differential Protection \(Merz-Price scheme\) and Buchholz Relay operation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Comprehensive Transformer Protection Schemes:) Tj
T*
(   1. Merz-Price Percentage Differential Protection:) Tj
T*
(   - Primary Protection against phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground winding short-circuits.) Tj
T*
(   - Operating Principle: Circulating current scheme comparing primary current I1 and secondary) Tj
T*
(   current I2 through current transformers \(CTs\).) Tj
T*
(   - CT Connections: To counteract transformer phase shifts:) Tj
T*
(   * Delta-connected transformer windings use STAR-connected CTs.) Tj
T*
(   * Star-connected transformer windings use DELTA-connected CTs.) Tj
T*
(   - Restraining and Operating Coils:) Tj
T*
(   Operating current: I_op = | I_ct1 - I_ct2 |.) Tj
T*
(   Restraining current: I_res = \( |I_ct1| + |I_ct2| \) / 2.) Tj
T*
(   Trip Condition: I_op > k * I_res \(where k is bias setting 10-40%\). Prevents false tripping due) Tj
T*
(   to CT saturation and tap-changer deviations.) Tj
T*
(   2. Buchholz Relay Protection:) Tj
T*
(   - Installed in pipe connecting transformer main oil tank to conservator tank.) Tj
T*
(   - Incipient / Minor Faults \(Local overheating, insulation breakdown\): Generates small gas) Tj
T*
(   bubbles. Gas accumulates in relay chamber, displacing oil downward; top float tilts and closes) Tj
T*
(   alarm contact.) Tj
T*
(   - Severe Internal Short-Circuit Faults: Violent arcing vaporizes oil, creating a high-velocity) Tj
T*
(   surge of oil toward conservator. Surging oil deflects bottom flap, immediately closing trip) Tj
T*
(   contacts to disconnect circuit breakers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 30:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Symmetrical 3-Phase Short Circuit on an Alternator, deriving subtransient, transient, and steady-state reactances.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Symmetrical 3-Phase Short-Circuit on Alternator:) Tj
T*
(   1. Transient Physical Process:) Tj
T*
(   - When a 3-phase dead short-circuit occurs at alternator terminals, armature current surges) Tj
T*
(   violently.) Tj
T*
(   - Magnetic flux cannot change instantaneously \(Constant Flux Linkage Theorem\).) Tj
T*
(   - Current envelope exhibits three distinct sequential time regimes:) Tj
T*
(   2. Three Reactance Regimes:) Tj
T*
(   - Subtransient Period \(First 1 to 3 cycles, 0.02 - 0.05 s\):) Tj
T*
(   Damper windings on pole shoes and solid rotor body oppose armature flux penetration. Flux is) Tj
T*
(   forced into high-reluctance leakage paths.) Tj
T*
(   Equivalent reactance is minimum: Direct-axis Subtransient Reactance X''d.) Tj
T*
(   Peak symmetrical short-circuit current: I'' = Eg / X''d \(highest current magnitude\).) Tj
T*
(   - Transient Period \(Next 10 to 30 cycles, 0.5 - 1.0 s\):) Tj
T*
(   Currents induced in damper windings decay to zero according to damper time constant.) Tj
T*
(   Flux now penetrates rotor field winding; field flux opposes further change.) Tj
T*
(   Equivalent reactance: Direct-axis Transient Reactance X'd.) Tj
T*
(   Current: I' = Eg / X'd.) Tj
T*
(   - Steady-State Period \(t > 1 to 2 seconds\):) Tj
T*
(   Transients in field circuit die away. Full armature reaction takes effect.) Tj
T*
(   Equivalent reactance: Direct-axis Synchronous Reactance Xd.) Tj
T*
(   Steady-state current: I_ss = Eg / Xd.) Tj
T*
(   3. Hierarchy of Reactances and Currents:) Tj
T*
(   - Reactances: X''d < X'd < Xd.) Tj
T*
(   - Currents: I'' > I' > I_ss.) Tj
T*
ET

